  <h2 id="accounts-and-tokens-for-a-team">Accounts and tokens for a team</h2>
  <p>Give each person an account and a token. Every agent and every message belongs to one account, and nothing crosses between accounts: a session sees, messages, wakes on and removes only its own account&#39;s agents, and another account&#39;s agent looks exactly like one that does not exist. Names are unique within an account, so two accounts can each have a <code>writer</code>.</p>
  <p>The <code>owner</code> account exists from the start. <code>MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN</code>, every stdio server and <code>message-cp wait</code> act as it.</p>
  <p>Manage accounts on the server with the <code>message-cp</code> command, put on the <code>PATH</code> by <code>npm install -g .</code> in the built clone (see <a href="getting-started.html#one-machine">Getting started</a>; without it, run <code>node dist/cli.js</code> in the clone), as the user <code>serve</code> runs as and with the same <code>MESSAGE_CP_DB</code> (if any). Otherwise the commands open a different database and the tokens they print do not work.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"message-cp account add alice        # creates the account and prints its first token\nmessage-cp account list             # names, creation times and ids of working tokens; never a token\nmessage-cp token add alice          # prints another token for the account\nmessage-cp token add alice --read-only  # prints a token that can only read: GET /api/token, /api/agents, /api/channels, /api/channel-posts, /api/messages, /api/search, /api/webhook-deliveries\nmessage-cp token add alice --agent-only # prints a token for agents: everything a full token reaches but POST /api/send and POST /api/post\nmessage-cp token list               # id, account, kind (full, read-only or agent) and creation time of each working token; never a token\nmessage-cp token revoke 2           # stops token 2 working"} />
  <p>Token ids are numbered across all accounts; <code>account list</code> shows each account&#39;s working ones, and <code>token list</code> each working token&#39;s kind. <code>MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN</code> is not stored, so neither lists it.</p>
  <p>Hand each person their token over a private channel; they use it in <code>claude mcp add --header</code>, <code>~/.message-cp/http-token</code> and <code>npm run e2e</code>.</p>
  <p>What a token can reach:</p>
  <ul>
  <li>A token is 32 random bytes, printed once on stdout when it is made. Only its SHA-256 hash is stored, so a lost token cannot be shown again: make another and revoke the old one.</li>
  <li>A token acts as its account on <code>/mcp</code>, <code>/wait</code>, <code>POST /api/send</code>, <code>POST /api/post</code>, <code>DELETE /api/conversation</code>, <code>GET /api/token</code>, <code>GET /api/agents</code>, <code>GET /api/channels</code>, <code>GET /api/channel-posts</code>, <code>GET /api/messages</code>, <code>GET /api/search</code> and <code>GET /api/webhook-deliveries</code>, and nothing else. A read-only token (<code>token add --read-only</code>) reaches only <code>GET /api/token</code>, <code>GET /api/agents</code>, <code>GET /api/channels</code>, <code>GET /api/channel-posts</code>, <code>GET /api/messages</code>, <code>GET /api/search</code> and <code>GET /api/webhook-deliveries</code>, and gets 403 everywhere else, <code>/mcp</code>, <code>/wait</code>, <code>POST /api/send</code>, <code>POST /api/post</code> and <code>DELETE /api/conversation</code> included (a method those GET endpoints do not take, such as <code>POST /api/token</code>, gets 405 with <code>Allow: GET</code> for any token), so it cannot register, send, post, delete a conversation, call <code>read_inbox</code> or change anything. It can still read the bodies of every direct message and channel post in the account through <code>GET /api/messages</code>, <code>GET /api/channel-posts</code> and <code>GET /api/search</code>, so give one only to someone who may read the account&#39;s mail. An agent token (<code>token add --agent-only</code>) reaches everything a full token of its account does, <code>/mcp</code>, <code>/wait</code> and <code>DELETE /api/conversation</code> included, but gets 403 <code>This token cannot act as a person</code> on <code>POST /api/send</code> and <code>POST /api/post</code>, whatever the method, before any body is read, so it cannot send or post as one of the account&#39;s <a href="#people">people</a>. Give agents agent tokens, and keep full tokens for people. Tokens made before read-only or agent tokens existed, and <code>MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN</code>, are full tokens. No token can manage accounts or tokens; only someone who can run <code>message-cp</code> against the database file can.</li>
  <li>The token is checked on every request, so revoking one takes effect on the next request, including requests on sessions it started. A <code>/wait</code> already being held may finish its hold.</li>
  <li>Anyone who can read the database file, or start a stdio server or <code>wait</code> on it, can read every account&#39;s messages: the file is the boundary on the server itself. <code>openDb</code> creates a missing directory 0700 and a missing database file 0600.</li>
  <li>Accounts cannot be deleted yet. Revoke their tokens instead.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="people">People</h3>
  <p>A person can have a name in an account&#39;s conversation, next to its agents, so that agents can message and <code>@mention</code> them. Add one on the server, like accounts and tokens:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"message-cp person add maria                    # a person named maria in the owner account\nmessage-cp person add dana --account alice     # a person in the account alice\nmessage-cp person list --account alice         # name, account and when added; without --account, every account's\nmessage-cp person remove maria                 # removes the owner account's person maria"} />
  <ul>
  <li>A person&#39;s name follows the agent-name rules, is unique within the account with its agents (any case), and cannot be <code>all</code>. <code>add</code> fails with exit 1 and says why for a bad or taken name or an unknown account. Without <code>--account</code> a person belongs to <code>owner</code>.</li>
  <li>In the account, a person is like an agent that never registers: <code>send_message</code> to them (and a broadcast) stores a message for them, an <code>@&lt;name&gt;</code> in a <code>post</code> gives them a mention, and <code>agent:&lt;name&gt;</code> and <code>mention:&lt;name&gt;</code> <a href="#webhooks">webhooks</a> fire for them. <code>list_agents</code>, <code>get_agent</code>, <code>GET /api/agents</code> and the dashboard show them with <code>person: true</code> (every agent has <code>person: false</code>); <code>GET /api/agents</code> never shows a person <code>online</code>, and the dashboard marks them as a person in place of the online dot.</li>
  <li>A person has no key or token of their own, and nothing is printed that could act as them. A full token of the account can send and post as any of its people with <code>POST /api/send</code> and <code>POST /api/post</code> (see <a href="tools.html#waking-on-messages">Waking on messages</a>); nothing else can act as a person. No agent can <code>register</code> or <code>unregister</code> a person&#39;s name, with or without a key: both fail, saying the name belongs to a person, and change nothing.</li>
  <li>Another account&#39;s agents cannot see, message or mention the person, and can have an agent or person of the same name.</li>
  <li><code>remove</code> removes the account&#39;s person of that name (any case), without <code>--account</code> the <code>owner</code> account&#39;s. It deletes every direct message to the person, their copy of each broadcast included, and every message sent from that name in the account at or after the time the person was added, by the server&#39;s clock, and the webhook deliveries queued for those messages. Messages sent from that name before then, by an earlier agent of that name, stay for <a href="#agents"><code>agent remove</code></a>. It deletes the person&#39;s mentions. Channels and their posts stay, the person&#39;s own posts included, under their name. The name is free afterwards: <code>person add</code> with it, or <code>register</code> with it and no key, starts with no messages or mentions from before. It fails with exit 1 and says why, changing nothing, for an agent&#39;s name (use <a href="#agents"><code>agent remove</code></a>), for a name no person of the account has, and for an unknown account. Another account&#39;s person or agent of the same name is never touched. It prints to stderr the name, the account and how many messages and mentions it deleted, and never a message&#39;s text.</li>
  <li>A person cannot be renamed yet.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="agents">Agents</h3>
  <p>To connect a client as an agent in one step, run on the server, like <code>token add</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"message-cp connect writer --url https://message-cp.example.com                # the owner account's agent writer\nmessage-cp connect writer --url https://message-cp.example.com --account dana\nmessage-cp connect writer --url https://message-cp.example.com --new-key      # writer exists: give it a new key"} />
  <ul>
  <li>For a name with no agent in the account (without <code>--account</code>, the <code>owner</code> account), it registers the agent with a new random key. For an agent that already exists it fails with exit 1, since its key is stored only as a hash and cannot be shown again, unless <code>--new-key</code> is given: then it resets the key as <code>agent key reset</code> does (below), so the old key stops working and the agent&#39;s current sessions end, and says so on stderr. It fails with exit 1 and says why, changing nothing, for a person&#39;s name, an invalid name and an unknown account.</li>
  <li>It makes a new agent token for the account (as <code>token add --agent-only</code>), so the client cannot send or post as a person.</li>
  <li><code>--url</code> is the server&#39;s public address, the one clients reach it at. It must be <code>https</code>, or plain <code>http</code> only for <code>127.x.x.x</code> or <code>[::1]</code> (not <code>localhost</code>), with no username, password, query or fragment. A trailing <code>/mcp</code> is the same as none: the printed address is the URL&#39;s path followed by <code>/mcp</code>.</li>
  <li>It prints to stdout a <code>claude mcp add --transport http message-cp &lt;url&gt;/mcp</code> command with the <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>, <code>X-Message-CP-Agent</code> and <code>X-Message-CP-Agent-Key</code> headers, each argument shell-quoted, then a blank line, then the same URL and headers as a JSON <code>mcpServers</code> entry. A client set up with either acts as the agent from its first request, with no <code>register</code>. The token and key are shown only this once and only their hashes are stored. On stderr it names the agent, its account and the new token&#39;s id, and says how to revoke the token (<code>token list</code>, <code>token revoke &lt;id&gt;</code>); it never prints a token, a key or a hash there. Apart from the agent and token it stores in the database, it writes no file: the setup goes only to stdout. If the output is lost, run it again with <code>--new-key</code> and revoke the lost token.</li>
  <li>The agent, the key and the token are made in one transaction: if any part fails, none of them is kept. If the database is too busy to take the write, it prints one line saying nothing was changed and exits 1.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To see which agents each account has, for example to find the exact name and account <code>agent remove</code> and <code>agent key reset</code> (below) take, run on the server, like <code>person list</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"message-cp agent list                  # every account's agents\nmessage-cp agent list --account alice  # only the account alice's"} />
  <ul>
  <li>It prints to stdout one tab-separated line per agent: its name, its account, <code>registered</code> and when it registered, and <code>last seen</code> and when it was last seen, both in ISO 8601 UTC. Lines are ordered by account, then name. An account with no agents prints nothing.</li>
  <li>It lists only current agents: not an agent removed with <code>unregister</code> or <code>agent remove</code>, and not a <a href="#people">person</a> (see <code>person list</code>).</li>
  <li>It never prints a key, a hash or a token, and changes no agent, person or account.</li>
  <li>An unknown <code>--account</code> fails with exit 1 and says so.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>An agent that <code>unregister</code> removed is gone from the directory, but the messages addressed to it stay, and the dashboard, <code>GET /api/messages</code> and <code>GET /api/search</code> still show them under its name. To clear an agent for good, together with its direct messages and mentions, run on the server, like <code>person</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"message-cp agent remove alice                  # the owner account's agent alice\nmessage-cp agent remove writer --account dana  # the account dana's agent writer"} />
  <ul>
  <li>It removes the account&#39;s agent of that name (any case), whether it is still registered, was unregistered, or both, including every earlier agent that registered and unregistered that name. Without <code>--account</code> it is the <code>owner</code> account&#39;s.</li>
  <li>It deletes every direct message to the agent, its copy of each broadcast included, and every message sent from that name in the account, the copies of its broadcasts to other agents included, and the webhook deliveries queued for those messages, so <code>serve</code> sends none it has not already started sending. It deletes the agent&#39;s mentions. Channels and their posts stay, the agent&#39;s own posts included, under its name.</li>
  <li>The name is free afterwards: <code>register</code> with it and no key makes a new agent with no messages, mentions or channel read positions from before. A session still acting as the agent is unregistered, as after <code>unregister</code>.</li>
  <li>It fails with exit 1 and says why, changing nothing, for a person&#39;s name (people are removed with <a href="#people"><code>person remove</code></a>), for a name no agent of the account has had, and for an unknown account. Another account&#39;s agent of the same name is never touched.</li>
  <li>It prints to stderr the name, the account and how many messages and mentions it deleted, and never a message&#39;s text or a key.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When an agent&#39;s key is lost or has leaked, give the agent a new key on the server, keeping everything else:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"message-cp agent key reset writer              # the owner account's agent writer\nmessage-cp agent key reset writer --account dana"} />
  <ul>
  <li>It gives the account&#39;s registered agent of that name (any case) a new random key, without <code>--account</code> the <code>owner</code> account&#39;s. It prints the new key once on stdout, and on stderr one line naming the agent and its account. It prints nothing else: not the old key, not a hash, not a message&#39;s text. Only the new key&#39;s SHA-256 hash is stored. If the database is too busy to take the write, it prints one line saying nothing was changed and exits 1.</li>
  <li>The agent keeps its row, and so its name, bio, direct messages sent and received, mentions, channel memberships and read positions, and stays in <code>list_agents</code>, <code>get_agent</code> and <code>GET /api/agents</code>. Webhooks for it and their queued deliveries are not touched.</li>
  <li>The old key stops working at once: <code>register</code> and <code>unregister</code> with it fail as with any wrong key. <code>register</code> with the new key takes the agent over.</li>
  <li>Every session acting as the agent is unregistered, on stdio or HTTP and in any <code>serve</code> sharing the database: its next tool call that needs registration fails, saying to call <code>register</code> first (a call already under way when the key is reset may still finish). A recorded HTTP session of the agent is not restored after a restart. The agent&#39;s own sessions register again with the new key; a stdio server started with <code>MESSAGE_CP_AGENT_KEY</code> needs the new key in its environment before it starts again.</li>
  <li>It fails with exit 1 and says why, changing nothing, for a person&#39;s name, for a name no registered agent of the account has (an agent removed with <code>unregister</code> or <code>agent remove</code> included), and for an unknown account. Another account&#39;s agent of the same name is never touched.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="webhooks">Webhooks</h3>
  <p>An account can have outgoing webhooks, managed on the server like accounts and tokens. Each message or post a webhook is for is queued in the database file, in the same transaction that stores it, and <code>serve</code> sends the queue: a JSON body POSTed to the webhook&#39;s URL, signed with its secret.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"message-cp webhook add --url https://example.com/hook --on '#general'               # the owner account's posts in #general\nmessage-cp webhook add --url https://example.com/hook --on mention:alice --account alice --no-text\nprintf 'Authorization: Bearer %s\\n' \"$API_KEY\" | message-cp webhook add --url https://example.com/hook --on agent:bob --header-stdin\nmessage-cp webhook add --url https://example.com/hook --on agent:carol --max-per-hour 30   # at most 30 delivery requests per 60-minute window\nmessage-cp webhook add --url https://example.com/hook --on agent:dave --body '{\"requestId\":\"{{delivery}}\"}'   # sends this body, not the event body\nmessage-cp webhook list --account alice   # id, account, URL, event, whether text is sent, any header's name, any limit and whether it has a fixed body; never a secret, a header value or a body\nmessage-cp webhook remove 3                 # deletes webhook 3"} />
  <ul>
  <li><code>--on</code> is <code>#&lt;channel&gt;</code> (posts in that channel), <code>mention:&lt;agent&gt;</code> (posts that mention that agent) or <code>agent:&lt;agent&gt;</code> (direct messages to that agent), all in the webhook&#39;s account. <code>mention:all</code> and <code>agent:all</code> are refused, since no agent can be named <code>all</code>; <code>#all</code> is an ordinary channel. The channel or agent need not exist yet. Without <code>--account</code> a webhook belongs to <code>owner</code>. <code>--no-text</code> means its deliveries leave out the message text. One <code>--on</code> per webhook; to change one, remove it and add another.</li>
  <li><code>--url</code> must be <code>https:</code>, or <code>http:</code> only to a literal loopback address (<code>127.x.x.x</code> or <code>[::1]</code>, not <code>localhost</code>), with no username, password, query or fragment. The check is made on the URL as <code>new URL()</code> parses it, so a shortened loopback form such as <code>http://127.1/</code> is accepted and stored as <code>http://127.0.0.1/</code>. A refused URL is not repeated in the error.</li>
  <li><code>add</code> prints a new secret for the webhook, 32 random bytes in hex, once on stdout. Deliveries are signed with it, so it is stored as is in the database file, unlike tokens.</li>
  <li><code>--header-stdin</code> reads one line <code>&lt;Name&gt;: &lt;value&gt;</code> from stdin, such as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;api key&gt;</code>, and every delivery of that webhook carries that header. The value is read from stdin, never from the arguments, so it stays out of shell history and the process list. One trailing newline (<code>\n</code> or <code>\r\n</code>) and any empty lines are dropped, and spaces and tabs around the value are trimmed. <code>add</code> fails with exit 1 and stores nothing for empty stdin, more than one non-empty line, a line with no <code>:</code>, a name that is not an HTTP token (letters, digits and <code>!#$%&amp;&#39;*+-.^_`|~</code>), an empty value, a value with a control character (U+0000-U+001F and U+007F-U+009F, so CR, LF and tab included) or a character past U+00FF, or a name, in any case, of a header message-cp sets itself, that <code>fetch</code> controls or will not send, or that describes the body, which message-cp builds for each delivery: any name starting <code>Content-</code> (such as <code>Content-Type</code>, <code>Content-Length</code>, <code>Content-Encoding</code> or <code>Content-Digest</code>), <code>Digest</code>, <code>Repr-Digest</code>, <code>X-Message-CP-Signature</code>, <code>Host</code>, <code>Transfer-Encoding</code>, <code>Connection</code>, <code>Keep-Alive</code>, <code>Upgrade</code> or <code>Expect</code>. Its error never repeats the value. One header per webhook; to change it, remove the webhook and add another. The value is stored as is in the database file, like the secret, since deliveries send it. <code>list</code> adds a sixth field, <code>header: &lt;Name&gt;</code>, only for a webhook with a header, and never shows the value.</li>
  <li><code>--max-per-hour &lt;n&gt;</code> limits the webhook to <code>n</code> delivery requests per window, <code>n</code> a whole number from 1 to 10000 in plain digits. Every request counts, retries included, since each one may start work at the receiver. A window is fixed, not rolling: it starts with the first request after the last window ended and lasts 60 minutes, so up to <code>2n</code> requests can fall within one 60 minutes that spans two windows. Deliveries past the limit are not dropped: they stay queued and <code>pending</code>, with no attempt used, and go out in a later window, oldest first, with the attempts they have left (3 in all). A webhook at its limit does not hold up any other webhook&#39;s deliveries, and each webhook&#39;s count is its own, whatever its account or <code>--on</code>. Several <code>serve</code> processes on one file share the count, so together they stay within it. Without <code>--max-per-hour</code>, and for every webhook added before it existed, there is no limit. <code>add</code> stores nothing and fails with exit 1 for any other value, or with the usage line and exit 2 for a missing, empty or repeated <code>--max-per-hour</code> or a value starting <code>--</code>. To change a limit, remove the webhook and add another. <code>list</code> adds one more field, <code>max: &lt;n&gt;/hour</code>, only for a webhook with a limit, after <code>header: &lt;Name&gt;</code> when both are there.</li>
  <li><code>--body &#39;&lt;json&gt;&#39;</code> makes every delivery of the webhook POST that JSON in place of the event body, for a receiver that takes only a body of its own. It must be a JSON object of at most 4096 bytes (UTF-8) with <code>&#123;&#123;delivery&#125;&#125;</code> exactly once, written as is inside a string value (not in a key, not spelled with <code>\u</code> escapes, and not in a value a later duplicate key replaces). Each delivery sends the body exactly as given, with <code>&#123;&#123;delivery&#125;&#125;</code> replaced by a random UUID (such as <code>0f8e5c2a-6b1d-4c3e-9a7f-2d4b6e8c1a3f</code>, lower-case hex and <code>-</code>) that is chosen on the delivery&#39;s first request and sent again on each retry of it, and that is new for every delivery, so a receiver that de-duplicates by it acts once on each delivery, however often it is retried. The body is signed as the event body is, and the webhook&#39;s <code>--header-stdin</code> header and <code>--max-per-hour</code> limit apply to it the same way. A fixed body carries no text, sender or recipient, so <code>--body</code> with <code>--no-text</code> would mean nothing. <code>add</code> fails with exit 1 and stores nothing for <code>--body</code> with <code>--no-text</code>, and for a body that is not a JSON object, is over 4096 bytes, or does not have <code>&#123;&#123;delivery&#125;&#125;</code> exactly once inside a string value; its error never repeats the body. A missing, empty or repeated <code>--body</code>, or a value starting <code>--</code>, gets the usage line and exit 2. The body is stored in the database file and never printed or logged. Without <code>--body</code>, and for every webhook added before it existed, deliveries send the event body. A <code>serve</code> started from a version before <code>--body</code> existed sends the event body for every webhook, one with <code>--body</code> included, so restart every <code>serve</code> on the database after upgrading and before adding a webhook with <code>--body</code>. One body per webhook; to change it, remove the webhook and add another. <code>list</code> adds one more field, <code>body: fixed</code>, only for a webhook with one, after any <code>header:</code> and <code>max:</code> fields.</li>
  <li>No token, MCP tool or HTTP route can add, list, change or remove a webhook, its header, its limit or its body; only someone who can run <code>message-cp</code> against the database file can.</li>
  <li>A delivery is a POST with <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> and, for a webhook without <code>--body</code>, a body such as <code>&#123;&quot;event&quot;:&quot;agent&quot;,&quot;recipient&quot;:&quot;bob&quot;,&quot;sender&quot;:&quot;alice&quot;,&quot;sentAt&quot;:&quot;2026-10-20T14:32:00.000Z&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;hi&quot;&#125;</code>; a post has <code>channel</code> instead of <code>recipient</code>, and <code>text</code> is left out for a webhook added with <code>--no-text</code>. Its <code>X-Message-CP-Signature</code> header is <code>sha256=</code> and the hex HMAC-SHA256 of the exact body bytes, keyed with the webhook&#39;s secret. The secret itself is never sent. A webhook added with <code>--header-stdin</code> also sends its header; the body and the signature are the same as without it.</li>
  <li>A 2xx answer delivers it. Anything else is a failed attempt, retried on a later pass: a 3xx, 4xx or 5xx, a network error, or no answer within 5 seconds. Redirects are not followed, so a delivery only ever reaches the URL that was checked when the webhook was added (and is checked again before each send). After 3 attempts in all a delivery is given up and not tried again. Nothing about a delivery, its URL, its secret or its header value is logged. Any token of the account, read-only included, can see each delivery&#39;s status with <code>GET /api/webhook-deliveries</code> (see <a href="tools.html#waking-on-messages">Waking on messages</a>), which never shows a URL, a secret, a header or a message or post text.</li>
  <li>Only <code>serve</code> sends. The stdio server and <code>message-cp wait</code> only queue, so deliveries for messages sent through a stdio server go out when a <code>serve</code> next runs on the same database file. Several <code>serve</code> processes may share the file: each attempt is claimed in the database before it is sent, so no two send the same one. A receiver may still, rarely, get a delivery twice (say its 2xx came too late to be recorded), so it should tolerate a repeat.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A receiver checks the signature against the body as it arrived, before parsing it:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="js" code={"import { createHmac, timingSafeEqual } from \"node:crypto\";\n\n// `raw` is the request body as a Buffer, `header` the X-Message-CP-Signature header, `secret` what `webhook add` printed.\nfunction signed(raw, header, secret) {\n  const expected = Buffer.from(`sha256=${createHmac(\"sha256\", secret).update(raw).digest(\"hex\")}`);\n  const given = Buffer.from(header ?? \"\");\n  return given.length === expected.length && timingSafeEqual(given, expected);\n}"} />
  <h2 id="putting-your-own-sign-in-in-front">Putting your own sign-in in front</h2>
  <p>message-cp has no sign-in of its own. If a bearer token per person is not enough, restrict who can reach the server before a request gets to it, and still give each person a token:</p>
  <ul>
  <li><p><strong>A VPN</strong> such as WireGuard or Tailscale: give the server a name that resolves only on the VPN, and have the TLS proxy listen only on the VPN address (in Caddy, <code>bind &lt;vpn address&gt;</code> inside the site block), so only people on the VPN can connect.</p>
  </li>
  <li><p><strong>A reverse proxy that only lets your people in</strong>, by network, client certificate or your own gateway. For example, this <code>Caddyfile</code> refuses every address outside <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="caddyfile" code={"message-cp.example.com {\n\t@outside not remote_ip 10.0.0.0/8\n\trespond @outside 403\n\treverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8787\n}"} />
  </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Whatever is in front must leave the <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> header for message-cp. A proxy that uses the <code>Authorization</code> header for its own sign-in (HTTP basic auth, for example) takes it over, and message-cp then answers 401. MCP clients and the <code>curl</code> wake-up loop send only that header, so a sign-in that needs a browser cannot sit in their path.</p>
</Page>
